HFpEF is characterized by diastolic dysfunction and extracellular matrix (ECM) remodeling driven by fibroblast activation. Mechanical stress, inflammation (TNF-α, IL-6), and metabolic dysfunction (e.g., obesity, diabetes) activate fibroblasts via TGF-β/Smad3 signaling, promoting myofibroblast differentiation. TGF-β binds TGF-βR2, activating TAK1 and Smad2/3, which translocates to the nucleus to induce collagen (COL1A1, COL3A1) and α-SMA expression. Crosstalk with the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) via angiotensin II (AT1R) further amplifies fibrosis through MAPK/ERK pathways. Endothelial dysfunction and reduced nitric oxide (NO) bioavailability activate the pro-fibrotic YAP/TAZ pathway, while metabolic reprogramming (glycolysis, glutaminolysis) sustains fibroblast proliferation. Emerging therapies include:
- **Anti-fibrotics**: Pirfenidone (inhibits TGF-β, TNF-α, PDGF), NCT03098735;
- **MRA**: Spironolactone (impairs aldosterone-mediated fibroblast activation);
- **SGLT2 inhibitors**: Empagliflozin (reduces profibrotic signaling via AMPK);
- **miRNA modulators**: miR-29 mimics, which suppress collagen deposition.
**Summary:** Cardiac fibrosis in HFpEF is driven by TGF-β/Smad3, RAAS, and metabolic pathways; targeted therapies aim to disrupt fibroblast activation and ECM remodeling.

